273:**PROVED external theorem, quoted with verified constants.** Reiher--Schoen, Theorem 1.2, states: if a finite nonempty subset B of any abelian group has additive energy E(B)>=|B| cubed/K, K>=1, then for each delta in (0,1/2) there is Bprime contained in B with
275:    |Bprime| >= (1-delta)|B|/sqrt(K),
276:    |Bprime-Bprime| <= 2^33 delta^(-9) K^4 |Bprime|.
280:**PROVED external theorem, quoted with its published dependence.** Green--Ruzsa, Theorem 1.1, states that |B+B|<=K|B| in any abelian group implies containment in a coset progression of dimension at most C K^4 log K and size at most exp(C K^4 log squared K)|B|, for an absolute constant C. See the authors' [paper](https://arxiv.org/html/math/0505198v1), Theorem 1.1. C is not numerically specified in that statement. It must not be replaced by an invented numerical constant. One may safely use log(2K) in the upper bounds to include K=1. The subgroup component is essential in a cyclic group; its order need not be bounded by D. Applying the standard sum/difference-set inequality |B+B|<=|B-B| cubed/|B| squared to the BSG output gives an available sum-doubling parameter at most (2^33 delta^(-9)K^4) cubed, before the Freiman bound. This already raises its rank dependence to an absolute constant times delta^(-108)K^48 times a logarithm.
282:**PROVED hypothesis audit / remaining gaps.** CY2 is not E(B)>=constant |B| cubed. To invoke those results requires: converting the weighted count into bounded-weight levels without losing all fixed mass; converting general j!=k relations to quadruple energy; and retaining the actual local binary pattern when passing to the extracted structure. A naive amplitude dyadic decomposition has up to 1+log2(n/H) levels, which grows with X; its largest level need carry only the reciprocal of this many levels of energy. Near-relations off a cyclic grid add a discretization issue. The exact cyclic reduction removes that last issue but does not remove the first three. No BSG invocation is justified solely by the word “structured.”
286:**HEURISTIC strategic decision after two attacks.** The finite rotation method advances the exact analytic inequality; the cyclic reduction advances its connection to P40. The inverse-theorem audit shows that neither “short relation” nor “fixed rank” completes the structured half. Discontinue attempts to get a proof by merely naming BSG/Freiman or by replacing the degree error with an unspecified o(1). Pursue a dimension-independent stability estimate retaining the binary bound. The next section proves the part of that estimate which is genuinely dimension independent and isolates the missing spectral completion.
288:## 5. QD-half: a dimension-independent stable Gram theorem
290:**PROVED stable defect lemma, new relative to the supplied record.** Let K be any compact abelian group. Let h be a continuous real function on R times K, with 0<=h<=1 and h(t,omega)=0 for |t|>1. Suppose h is the Fourier--Stieltjes transform of a finite real signed measure nu on the dual group; let delta be the total mass of its negative part. Put m=integral_(0<t<1,K) h and b=integral h(1-h). Then
292:    h(t,omega) <= 1-t^2/4+2delta, 0<|t|<=1,
293:    b >= m^3/12-2delta m.                               (SG)
295:There is no rank, torsion, or J in this statement. In particular delta<=m squared/48 implies b>=m cubed/24. The exact delta=0 case is the round-4 bound, quoted rather than claimed new.
297:**PROVED proof of SG.** Symmetrize the Jordan parts under inversion, which preserves positivity and their masses, so their transforms hplus,hminus are real positive-definite functions and |hminus|<=delta. Write a=hplus(0,e); since h(0,e)<=1, a<=1+delta. For z=(t,omega), let N=floor(1/|t|)+1, so h(Nz)=0 and hplus(Nz)=hminus(Nz)<=delta. The Gram triangle inequality for hplus gives
299:    a-hplus(z) >= [a-hplus(Nz)]/N^2 >= (a-delta)/N^2.
301:Consequently h(z)<=a(1-1/N squared)+delta/N squared+delta <=1-1/N squared+2delta <=1-t squared/4+2delta. Multiply 1-h>=t squared/4-2delta by h>=0 and integrate. Since 0<=integral_K h(t,omega)<=1 and its total mass is m, filling the earliest t first gives integral t squared h>=m cubed/3. This proves SG. The argument also handles a-delta<0; no division by it is made.
303:**PROVED quantitative consequence conditional on a binary spectral lift.** Suppose a finite reconstruction at complexity J produces such h with m=n/X>=1/C, negative spectral mass delta<=L_J M/X, and captured cosine energy <=(X/2)integral h squared. Then SG gives
309:**PROVED obstruction to the naive estimate.** A pointwise lower bound -M/X for each of J Fourier components on a y-interval of length 2B bounds their total negative spectral mass only by a constant times J B M/X. The Lebesgue length and the component count both have to be included. Cauchy--Schwarz does not automatically turn this into sqrt(J)M/X: the L2 norm of a function bounded by M/X on a set of measure proportional to J B already has the factor sqrt(J B), which is multiplied by sqrt(J B) in the L2-to-L1 conversion. Pointwise negativity bounds alone therefore retain a linear J loss. The old singular-profile and joint-PD examples remain pertinent to extraction; SG does not presume they are binary lifts.
311:**CONJECTURED single sufficient missing lemma, height-uniform binary spectral completion (HBSC), asymptotic form.** For every C>=1 there is B(C)<infinity with this property. For every sequence X_j tending to infinity, A_j contained in [1,X_j] with n_j>=X_j/C and M(A_j)/X_j tending to zero, every choice sqrt(n_j)<=H_j<=n_j, and every union U_j of at most ceil(3pi X_j n_j/H_j squared) radius-1/X_j windows with f_Aj(center)>H_j, there are compact abelian groups K_j and continuous functions h_j as in SG, with finite signed Fourier measures, for which
314:    delta_j <= B(C) M(A_j)/H_j + o(1),
317:All little-o terms refer to the chosen sequence and must hold despite its growing window count; no ineffective fixed-J substitution is allowed. This is one reconstruction lemma, not three independently proved facts. Its substantive requirements are retention of 0<=h<=1 and the negative spectral mass bound uniformly in H and the number of windows. Its scope is deliberately M=o(X), the regime needed for the contradiction. Requiring a continuous supported h of mass exactly n/X for EVERY A would already fail for the full interval n=X, since such an h cannot have mass 1. The asymptotic mass condition and the small-M hypothesis avoid that elementary false strengthening. HBSC is stronger than the needed asymptotic capture inequality and is not claimed equivalent to it. Empty unions and H>n need no completion, since their residual is the full n/2.
319:**PROVED HBSC implies HBCG asymptotically in the needed small-M/H regime, and hence T-dense.** By SG and the total cosine energy n/2, HBSC gives residual >=X/(24 C cubed)-B(C)XM/H-o(X) along sequences with bounded M/H. In a hypothetical counterexample sequence choose H=sqrt(n/epsilon), epsilon=M/sqrt(n) tending to zero, and use the separated-window cover from round 4. The quoted elementary lower bound M>=1/2 ensures H=o(X) and H<=n eventually, so the stated HBSC range applies. P40 gives residual o(X); HBSC gives a fixed positive multiple of X. This contradiction proves T-dense conditionally. The same reconstruction at measure mu, with delta bounded by a constant times M sqrt(mu/n) plus a vanishing error, would give QD-half for sufficiently small threshold constants. No such completion has been proved here.
321:**PROVED best unconditional dependence recorded.** The actual finite-window result remains: for each fixed C,J,K there exist eta(C,J,K)>0 and X0(C,J,K), with residual >=X/(48 C cubed). No explicit numerical rate of eta in J is proved in this report or in round 4. SG improves the stability mechanism but does not provide the missing finite lift. A multiscale iteration with a fixed loss per step remains inadequate; no summable-loss recurrence has been established. This is the exact nonclosure, rather than a claim that the existing compactness proof has an effective J-uniform rate.
